"A Kubernetes traffic-flow diagram traces a request through the selected entry, Service abstraction, endpoints, and workload path.\n\nUse when: Explain how external or internal requests reach Pods. Investigate mismatched ports, selectors, and traffic boundaries.\n\nAvoid when: The main question is controller ownership or source-module dependencies.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes distinguish ingress or gateway controllers, Services, EndpointSlices, and Pods. Directional paths identify request handling and relevant address or port translation. Boundaries identify cluster, namespace, and external network scope where needed.\n\nBuild: Choose one concrete request and networking implementation. Trace entry and Service selection from current configuration. Label service ports, target ports, and relevant endpoint membership. Keep control relationships separate from packet paths with clear padded boundaries.\n\nCheck: The diagram does not imply the abstract Service is always a packet-processing process. Ports and selectors match the selected configuration. Network-policy and implementation assumptions are explicit. Packet arrows avoid port labels and workload descriptions.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming every cluster uses the same proxy or ingress implementation. Presenting EndpointSlice membership as controller ownership.\n\nExample: An HTTPS request reaches a gateway controller, then a Service-selected API endpoint on its configured target port.\n\nAlternatives: Kubernetes ownership diagram, Logical network diagram."
